								<div class="title" id="post-title-2378000">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608563773" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442611975 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2015 21:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2378000">
									

<p>Was wondering when this was going up. I'll be self-serving for a moment and say that you picked a good time to do it.</p>
<p>Ordinarily, I have no love for the form of narrative where the person in the know tells someone (sometimes the reader directly) How it Is. Those things <em>tend</em> to be more worldbuilding supplement than story. Not impossible to make something like that work, but a writer is swimming against a strong current when they do so.</p>
<p>This, however, is a nice twist on that method of narrative. The message of course resonates with me, but that isn't the reason that I enjoy this. The story is really more about the feelings of this older artist as he dispenses wisdom while at the same time examining what the real worth is in the act of giving it to the younger person. It's interesting to see a mentor figure feel things and examine his own words even as he gives them in the course of delivering what he has to say, and I'm not sure that I've seen anything quite like it on the site. This is a great counterpoint to one of my least favorite article archetypes, the Orientation.</p>
<p>Best of all, this is a believable character. The way experience informs his observations and his thoughts rings very true to me, and you manage to make a little piece of who this person is come through in his narrative, as well as the world in which he lives.</p>
<p>Excellent work.</p>

							</div>

								
					
