					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=348648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608563773" alt="sirpudding" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=348648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;" >sirpudding</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442612424 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2015 21:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2378003">
									

<p>I think this succeeds precisely because the mentor is self aware of the necessary hypocrisy of mentoring, and the teacher is clearly in turn also taught. There's also the sense, that speaks to me personally, of finding that somehow while you weren't paying attention, the giants of your youth have faded and somehow you of all people are supposed to fill their role but you don't feel like a giant, you feel like you.</p>

							</div>

								
					
