					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608563773" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442612914 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2015 21:48</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2378008">
									

<p>Hits all of the right notes and does it in a really compelling (for the kind of storytelling you're embarking on) way, certainly moreso than other stories that try to take this kind of course. But it's hard for me to upvote something that ultimately feels as slow as a snail's pace due to the amount of talking and narrating from one specific person, rather than actually seeing the action going on. Even if it's well-written, it can't keep my attention for long before I tab out to do other stuff, and I feel like I'm at a college lecture taking notes with the occasional slideshow, rather than seeing any of the things the teacher wants to impart on me.</p>

							</div>

								
